<h2 class="ds-section-title">Requirements</h2>

<div class="ds-callout">
  <div class="ds-limits">
    <p class="ds-intro-text">
      <strong>Engines:</strong> PostgreSQL 14 or newer, MySQL 5.7 or newer, ClickHouse 20.1 or newer. Pro accepts any
      pairing of the three.
    </p>
    <p class="ds-intro-text">
      <strong>Table:</strong> one integer primary key named <code>id</code>, with the same table name and column order
      on both sides.
    </p>
    <p class="ds-intro-text">
      <strong>Run:</strong> a differing segment is read whole, and the comparison is not a transaction. Pin the session
      timezone and charset on both connections.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="ds-section-title">Algorithm notes</h2>

<div class="ds-algo-notes">
  <p class="ds-algo-lede">
    DB Diff does the comparison inside the database. The wire carries checksums when the tables match, and rows only
    when they do not.
  </p>

  <ol class="ds-algo-steps">
    <li class="ds-algo-step">
      <span class="ds-algo-step-number">01</span>
      <h3 class="ds-algo-step-title">Segment the table</h3>
      <p class="ds-algo-step-text">
        The table is cut into ranges of primary keys, 10,000 by default. Both sides can address the same range without
        moving a row.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="ds-algo-step">
      <span class="ds-algo-step-number">02</span>
      <h3 class="ds-algo-step-title">Checksum each segment</h3>
      <p class="ds-algo-step-text">
        Each side hashes every compared column on its own, folds the digests with XOR, and returns one 64-bit checksum.
        Equal checksums skip the segment.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="ds-algo-step">
      <span class="ds-algo-step-number">03</span>
      <h3 class="ds-algo-step-title">Read only what differs</h3>
      <p class="ds-algo-step-text">
        A differing segment is read row by row and compared column by column. The output is the primary keys that
        changed.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="ds-algo-code">
    <span class="ds-algo-code-label">Row checksum</span>
    <pre><code>md5( null_bitmap || md5(COALESCE(col_a, '')) || md5(COALESCE(col_b, '')) || ... ) -> first 8 bytes
segment = XOR(row checksums)</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="ds-algo-foot">
    <p class="ds-algo-note">
      Across engines, the checksum needs a canonical form: PostgreSQL writes a decimal as <code>1.50</code> and
      ClickHouse writes it as <code>1.5</code>. Pro reduces every value to a form all three engines agree on before it
      is hashed. A NULL bitmap sits in front of the column digests, so a NULL and an empty string cannot hash alike.
    </p>
    <a
      href="https://github.com/clubmatto/vetrina/blob/main/db-diff/docs/how-it-works.md"
      class="ds-btn ds-btn--primary"
      >Read the full algorithm notes</a
    >
  </div>
</div>

